WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.350
A scam in our staff group — and my phone

2
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.450
already flagged one just like it as spam.

3
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.540
How did it learn to do that?

4
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.180
Today: how machines learn from

5
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.420
examples — step by step.

6
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:15.920
It starts with people.

7
00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.530
They collect examples — for a spam filter,

8
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.510
lots of emails — and give each one a

9
00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:26.110
label, the right answer: spam, or not

10
00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:26.330
spam.

11
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.000
These labelled examples are

12
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:29.920
the training data.

13
00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.550
Next, training.

14
00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:35.190
The computer searches the labelled

15
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.110
examples for patterns — like words and

16
00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.250
requests that keep appearing in spam:

17
00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.810
“confirm your PIN”, “act today”.

18
00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.270
What it finds is stored as a

19
00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.670
model: patterns kept as numbers.

20
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.340
Then we test the model on labelled

21
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.920
examples it has never seen, and check

22
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.800
its guesses against the real labels.

23
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.360
Too many wrong?

24
00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.010
People add more examples — and more

25
00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.390
varied ones — and train it again.

26
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.880
So, machine learning is a way of

27
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.200
building AI where the computer finds

28
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.700
patterns in examples, instead of a

29
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.080
person writing every rule.

30
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:16.600
Five steps: collect examples, label

31
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.360
them, train, test — then use the

32
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:21.460
model to make predictions.

33
00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.330
It’s like teaching a younger

34
00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.690
pupil to pick ripe mangoes.

35
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.260
You show many mangoes and say

36
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.640
“ripe” or “not ripe”.

37
00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.750
They notice patterns — colour, softness,

38
00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.310
smell — and you correct their mistakes.

39
00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.690
Then you test them with

40
00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.310
mangoes they’ve never seen.

41
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:44.160
Quick check!

42
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.330
Why do we test a model on

43
00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:49.670
examples it has never seen?

44
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.500
To check it learned patterns,

45
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.180
not just its examples.

46
00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.870
A list of blocked numbers only

47
00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.570
stops numbers someone added.

48
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.560
A model that learned patterns can often

49
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.600
catch a new scam it has never seen.

50
00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.070
You’ll find these steps in many tools.

51
00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:10.590
One cassava app was trained on thousands

52
00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:13.330
of leaf photos, each labelled by experts.

53
00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:16.290
One voice-typing tool learned from

54
00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:17.970
recordings of native speakers.

55
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.280
And one flood-forecasting system learned

56
00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.900
from records of thousands of river gauges.

57
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.440
In the app, after this video, you’ll

58
00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.220
train a tiny scam spotter: give it

59
00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.760
messages labelled safe or scam, then

60
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.620
test it on one it has never seen.

61
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:36.580
Your turn.

62
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.920
My phone marks a new text

63
00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.760
as “suspected spam”.

64
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:42.340
Is that a prediction?

65
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.140
Yes — “Is this spam?”

66
00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.490
is a prediction about now.

67
00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.230
Remember: collect and label examples,

68
00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:53.150
train a model, test it on new ones,

69
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:54.990
then use it to make predictions.

70
00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.500
And many models stay the same

71
00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.540
until people retrain them.

72
00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.010
Try it in class tomorrow:

73
00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:04.150
play “Be the machine”.

74
00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.700
Draw shapes that are zogs and

75
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:07.600
shapes that are not.

76
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:09.920
Pupils find the hidden pattern,

77
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.800
then predict new shapes — the same

78
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.480
steps a spam filter follows.
